findings.evidence:
The `try/catch` around the capture call has an **empty catch block**, so a declined card resolves as success upstream:

```ts
try {
  await capture(intentId);
} catch {}          // ← decline is silently eaten
return { ok: true };
```

Callers can never distinguish a real capture from a declined one.

The empty `catch {}` at `src/pay/charge.ts:58` isn't just a bug risk — it breaks a rule this repo commits to in writing.

## The rule

`docs/conventions/error-handling.md` states, as a **MUST** (echoed in `AGENTS.md`): *"never swallow an error — re-throw, or return a typed failure. Empty catch blocks are not allowed."*

```ts
try {
  await capture(intentId);
} catch {}          // ← rule violation: silent swallow
```

## Why the repo has this rule

Swallowed errors are the hardest class of bug to trace in production: the failure is real, but no log, metric, or caller ever learns about it. The convention exists so every failure is either handled or propagated.

